              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the French for consequently, then the French for that is why."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: à cause de",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "à cause de — \"because of\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nous sommes restés à la maison à cause de la neige. We stayed at home because of the snow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Followed by a noun; with a verb, use parce que. À cause du, à cause des."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "à cause de is built on cause, which comes from Latin causa \"cause, reason, lawsuit\" (EtymDB; Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
        },
